# Howdy Notifications

`howdy_notifications` is an optional package for telling people that
something happened: a comment on their post, a report that finished, a
sign-in from a new device. Each notification is:

- kept in an inbox in your database until it is read
- pushed straight away to every page the recipient has open, in any tab
- emailed too, straight away or in a digest, if the kind calls for it
- shown by the browser itself with [Web Push](/docs/notifications/web-push),
  even when the app is closed

For feedback on what the user just did, such as "Settings saved" after a
form post, use [flash messages](/docs/controllers/flash-messages) instead.

## Install

Add the package to `gleam.toml`, next to `howdy`:

```toml
howdy_notifications = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "notifications" }
```

It keeps its tables in your app's database, through
[`howdy_database`](/docs/database/overview), on PostgreSQL or SQLite. Apply
its migrations with your own when you deploy:

```gleam
import howdy/migration
import howdy/notifications

migration.run(db, [my_app.schema(), notifications.schema()])
```

## Sending

Make one `Notifier` when the app starts and keep it:

```gleam
let notifier = notifications.new(db)
```

Then send from wherever something happens, once the change it is about has
been committed:

```gleam
notifications.draft("comment.created", "Ana commented on your post")
|> notifications.body("Looks good to me!")
|> notifications.link("/posts/42#comment-7")
|> notifications.data([#("post_id", "42")])
|> notifications.send(notifier, to: post.author_id)
```

The kind, here `comment.created`, is yours to choose: it is what users set
preferences for. The recipient is a user ID as a string, such as a
`howdy_auth` user's `id`. `send_to_each` sends the same notification to a
list of users.

The notification is saved first, then announced. Saving is what counts: a
page that was closed or offline reads the inbox when it next connects, so
nothing is missed.

## The inbox

The functions behind any inbox page:

- `notifications.list(notifier, user, notifications.page())` gives the
  newest twenty, newest first. `page() |> limit(50) |> before(id)` gives
  the next page, and `unread_only` only those not yet read.
- `notifications.unread_count(notifier, user)` gives the number for a badge.
- `mark_read`, `mark_unread`, `mark_all_read` and `delete` change it. A user
  can only see and change their own: anyone else's is `NotFound`.

`notifications.routes` serves all of this over HTTP, for the bell below or
a page of your own:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(
  notifications.routes(notifier, at: "/notifications", identify: fn(ctx) {
    auth.required(auth)(ctx) |> result.map(fn(principal) { principal.user.id })
  }),
)
```

`identify` names the signed-in user, or refuses the request. Writes come
from the user's browser with their cookie, so it must also refuse
cross-site requests; `auth.required` does. The routes are:

- `GET /notifications` lists the inbox as
  `{"notifications": [...], "unread": 3}`, taking `limit`, `before` and
  `unread=true` in the query
- `GET /notifications/events`, a stream of [server-sent events](/docs/controllers/server-sent-events)
- `POST /notifications/read` marks every notification read
- `POST /notifications/:id/read` and `POST /notifications/:id/unread`
- `DELETE /notifications/:id`

Writes answer `{"unread": 2}`. They also work as plain HTML forms: add
`?next=/inbox` to the action, and the answer is a redirect back there.

## Live

The event stream opens with the unread count, then sends each new
notification as it arrives and the count whenever it changes, from this tab
or any other:

```text
event: unread
data: {"count":3}

event: notification
data: {"id":"…","kind":"comment.created","title":"Ana commented on your post",…}
```

Announcements go through the same `pg` groups as
[channels](/docs/controllers/websockets), so they reach the recipient's
pages on every node of a connected cluster.

### The bell

`howdy_ui`'s [bell](/components/bell) is a button with the unread count,
whose panel lists the newest notifications. It follows the event stream by
itself, so the count changes and a toast appears as notifications arrive:

```gleam
bell.new(id: "inbox", source: "/notifications")
|> bell.unread(unread_count)
|> bell.inbox("/inbox")
|> bell.view
```

Give it the unread count as the page is drawn, so it is right before the
stream connects, and put it in the page itself, such as the header, rather
than in a live view. Opening the panel reads the list; clicking a
notification marks it read on the way to its link.

Each open tab with a bell holds a connection open. Browsers allow six
connections to a site at once over HTTP/1.1, so serve the app over HTTP/2,
as howdy does with TLS, if people keep many tabs open.

### In a live view

A live view, or any other process, can subscribe to a user's inbox and
hear `Received(notification)` and `Unread(count)` events:

```gleam
fn init(user_id: String) {
  #(model, server_component.select(fn(_dispatch, subject) {
    notifications.subscribe(user_id, subject)
    process.new_selector() |> process.select_map(subject, Notified)
  }))
}
```

The subscription ends when the live view does.

## Preferences

Each user chooses how each kind reaches them: in the inbox, by email
(straight away or in a digest), and by push. The app sets the defaults:

```gleam
notifications.with_defaults(notifier, fn(kind) {
  case kind {
    "security." <> _ -> Delivery(..default_delivery, email: EmailNow)
    "comment.created" -> Delivery(..default_delivery, email: EmailDigest)
    _ -> default_delivery
  }
})
```

`default_delivery` is the inbox and push, with no email. A user's choice
overrides it, for one kind or for every kind:

```gleam
notifications.set_preference(notifier, user_id, "comment.created", notifications.muted)
notifications.set_preference(notifier, user_id, notifications.every_kind,
  Delivery(inbox: True, email: EmailDigest, push: False))
```

`preferences` lists what a user chose, for a settings page, and
`clear_preference` goes back to the default. A muted kind is not saved at
all.

## Deleting accounts

`notifications.delete_user(conn, user_id)` removes everything kept for a
user. Give it `auth.with_account_deletion`'s transaction, so it goes with
the account. `prune(notifier, read_before:)` deletes read notifications
older than a time, to keep the table small.

## Next

[Email and digests](/docs/notifications/delivery) covers the other ways
out and the worker that sends them, and [Web Push](/docs/notifications/web-push)
the browser's own notifications. `examples/notifications` puts it all
together, with a flash message after each send.
